This is Erdős Problem 218, which is an open research problem in number theory. The three theorems stated are all marked `@[category research open]` — they are conjectures about the distribution of consecutive prime gaps:

1. The set of indices $n$ where $\text{primeGap}(n) \leq \text{primeGap}(n+1)$ has natural density $\frac{1}{2}$.
2. The set of indices $n$ where $\text{primeGap}(n+1) \leq \text{primeGap}(n)$ has natural density $\frac{1}{2}$.
3. There are infinitely many $n$ with $\text{primeGap}(n) = \text{primeGap}(n+1)$.

These are genuinely open conjectures. The first two together would imply that the set of equal consecutive prime gaps has natural density $0$ (since the two sets cover all indices and each has density $\frac{1}{2}$, their intersection has density $0$), while the third asserts this density-$0$ set is nonetheless infinite.

No proof of any of these statements is known. The distribution of consecutive prime gaps — in particular, whether they behave like i.i.d. random variables (which would heuristically give density $\frac{1}{2}$ for each comparison) — is far beyond current techniques. Even the weaker third statement (infinitely many equal consecutive prime gaps, equivalent to infinitely many 3-term arithmetic progressions of primes in a specific sense) is open.

